## Task tracking

This repo uses `todo-sqlite-cli` for TODOs. The DB path is resolved via the
`.todo-sqlite-cli` marker at the repo root (or `$TODO_SQLITE_CLI_DB`, or the
`--db` flag — in that precedence order).

> **If the MCP server is wired up** (`todo` in your MCP servers config), use
> the MCP tools (`list_tasks`, `add_task`, `start_task`, etc.) instead of the
> shell commands below. The tool descriptions carry the same invariants. The
> rest of this section is for direct CLI use.

**Before planning or coding, ask the DB — do not read a PLAN.md file:**

- `todo-sqlite-cli next` — the single task to work on right now. Returns
  the oldest in-progress task, then the oldest partial (paused) task, then
  the highest-priority unblocked pending task. Prefer this over `list` —
  it returns one task in one query. When no work remains, `next` prints
  nothing (or `null` under `--json`) and exits 0 — use that as a clean
  termination signal. `next` never surfaces a gate task (see **Gates**
  below); if only gates remain open it still reports nothing actionable,
  with a stderr hint pointing at `list --kind gate`.
- `todo-sqlite-cli list` — active tasks (in-progress + partial + pending),
  ordered by status then priority. Tabular output is already terse.
- `todo-sqlite-cli list --status partial` (or `pending` / `done` /
  `rejected` / `all`) — filter to one status when scanning a subset.
- `todo-sqlite-cli show <id>` — details for one task. Default output omits
  default-valued fields (`pending`, `P3`); pass `--verbose` for everything.
  Pass `--format markdown` for a human-readable markdown card.

**Cheap re-polling (token-frugal):**

- `todo-sqlite-cli list --ids-only` — just IDs, one per line. Cheapest way
  to detect change between turns. Combine with `--json` for an ID array.
- `todo-sqlite-cli list --since 2026-05-07` — only tasks created on/after a
  date, for incremental updates.
- `todo-sqlite-cli export-todo --format markdown` — terse markdown by
  default; pass `--verbose` only when you need every field.
- `todo-sqlite-cli export-completed` — compact JSON by default; pass
  `--pretty` only for human review; `--format markdown` for a date-grouped
  checklist.

**JSON output shape (important when piping to `jq` / Python):**

- `list --json` and `export-todo` JSON wrap tasks: `{"tasks": [...]}`.
  Unwrap with `jq '.tasks[]'` or `json.load(sys.stdin)["tasks"]`.
- `export-completed` JSON wraps and groups by date:
  `{"completed": [{"date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "tasks": [...]}, ...]}`.
- Single-task commands (`next --json`, `show --json`, `add --json`,
  `start/stop/done/revert/edit --json`) return a bare task object.
- For streaming pipelines, prefer `--format ndjson` on `list`,
  `export-todo`, or `export-completed` — one task object per line, no
  wrapper, no date grouping. Composes cleanly with `jq -c`, `grep`, `head`,
  and shell line counts.

**When picking up work:**

- `todo-sqlite-cli start <id>` before coding. If another task was already
  in-progress, it is automatically moved to `partial` (paused, with its
  `started_at` preserved). Pass `--force` to keep multiple tasks
  in-progress simultaneously.
- `todo-sqlite-cli stop <id>` to pause without committing — the task moves
  to `partial`, ready to be resumed via `start <id>`.
- `todo-sqlite-cli revert <id>` to discard the start: clears `started_at`
  and returns the task to `pending`.
- `todo-sqlite-cli done <id>` when the change is committed. Idempotent —
  safe to call twice. Pass `--rejected` to close it as `rejected`
  (declined / won't-do) instead of `done`; this still records
  `completed_at` but does NOT unblock tasks that depend on it.

**When a new task surfaces:**

- `todo-sqlite-cli add "title" --details "..." --tag <area> --priority <P>`
  where `<P>` is `1`-`5` or `P1`-`P5` (priority 1 is highest, 3 is default).
- `--depends-on <id>` on `add` to link a prerequisite at creation time.
  Tasks with unmet dependencies are skipped by `next` and flagged
  `[blocked]` in `list`. Prefer wiring deps at `add` time over editing
  later.

**Editing or annotating a task:**

- `todo-sqlite-cli edit <id> --append-details "note"` — appends a progress
  note to existing details with a newline separator. Use this for
  incremental status notes mid-task instead of `--details` (which
  replaces) so prior context isn't lost.
- `todo-sqlite-cli edit <id> --details "new body"` replaces details
  wholesale; `--clear-details` empties them. These three flags are
  mutually exclusive.
- `todo-sqlite-cli edit <id> --add-tag <t>` / `--rm-tag <t>` — manage tags
  (repeatable).
- `todo-sqlite-cli edit <id> --add-dep <other-id>` / `--rm-dep <id>` —
  manage dependency edges (repeatable; cycles are rejected).
- `todo-sqlite-cli edit <id> --title "..."` / `--priority P2` — rename or
  reprioritize.

**Gates — a task that's a checkpoint, not work:**

- Use `add "condition prose" --gate --details "..."` when the "task" is
  really "wait until X becomes true" (e.g. a dependency reaching
  maintenance-mode stability), not something to `start`/work incrementally.
  Promote or demote an existing task with `edit <id> --gate` /
  `--no-gate` (mutually exclusive).
- A gate is invisible to `next` and never flagged `[STALE]` by `aging`
  regardless of `--stale-days` — indefinite openness is the correct state
  for a gate, not backlog rot. `list`/`show`/`export-todo` prefix `[GATE]`
  before its title so it's visually distinct from a real open task; every
  task object (JSON/ndjson) carries the raw `is_gate` boolean.
- `list --kind gate` — a readiness dashboard of every open checkpoint (add
  `--status all` if you also want closed gates). `--kind task` excludes
  gates from a normal listing.

**Weekly rebase / backlog health:**

- `todo-sqlite-cli aging --stale-days 14` — open tasks (pending/partial/
  in-progress), oldest first, with `age_days` and a `stale` flag past the
  threshold. Read-only — it does not touch `priority` or `next`'s ordering.
  Use it to spot low-priority tasks aging unnoticed, then `edit --priority`
  the ones worth pulling forward. `--tag` filters to one area.
- `todo-sqlite-cli cfd --bucket week` — cumulative flow diagram data
  (backlog/in-progress/done/rejected counts per bucket), reconstructed
  from `created_at`/`started_at`/`completed_at` — no separate history
  table to keep in sync. `--format json` for `{"buckets": [...]}`;
  `--format csv` for spreadsheets/plotting. Use it to see whether the
  backlog is actually thinning or just churning between reverts.

**Statuses:** `pending` → `in-progress` → `done`. `partial` is the paused
state for a task that was started and then displaced (or stopped) — use
`start` to resume it, `revert` to discard the start. `rejected` is a
terminal "closed but not done" state (`done --rejected`) for declined or
won't-do tasks — unlike `done`, it does not satisfy dependency edges.

**Exit codes:** 0 success, 1 user error, 2 system error. Every command
supports `--json` and `--db PATH`.
